std.automata
************

Ready-made DFAs for common patterns (prefixes, suffixes, substrings, counting,
parity, lengths), and questions about regular languages: a shortest word, a word
that tells two languages apart, finiteness and counting words.

  fn starts_with(alphabet: Alphabet, prefix: Str) -> Dfa
      The smallest DFA for the strings over `alphabet` that begin with `prefix`.

  fn ends_with(alphabet: Alphabet, suffix: Str) -> Dfa
      The smallest DFA for the strings over `alphabet` that end with `suffix`.

  fn contains_substring(alphabet: Alphabet, part: Str) -> Dfa
      The smallest DFA for the strings over `alphabet` that contain `part`
      somewhere.

  fn exactly(alphabet: Alphabet, symbol: Str, n: Int) -> Dfa
      The smallest DFA for the strings with exactly `n` copies of `symbol`.

  fn at_least(alphabet: Alphabet, symbol: Str, n: Int) -> Dfa
      The smallest DFA for the strings with at least `n` copies of `symbol`.

  fn at_most(alphabet: Alphabet, symbol: Str, n: Int) -> Dfa
      The smallest DFA for the strings with at most `n` copies of `symbol`.

  fn count_mod(alphabet: Alphabet, symbol: Str, m: Int, r: Int) -> Dfa
      The smallest DFA for the strings whose number of `symbol`s leaves
      remainder `r` when divided by `m`; `m = 2` gives parity.

  fn length_mod(alphabet: Alphabet, m: Int, r: Int) -> Dfa
      The smallest DFA for the strings whose length leaves remainder `r` when
      divided by `m`.

  fn length_between(alphabet: Alphabet, min: Int, max: Int) -> Dfa
      The smallest DFA for the strings of length `min` to `max`, both included.

  fn shortest_word(lang: Lang) -> Str
      A shortest word of the language (the first in alphabet order if there are
      several); the language must not be empty.

  fn distinguishing_word(a: Lang, b: Lang) -> Str
      A shortest word in one language but not the other, showing why they are
      not `equivalent`; they must differ.

  fn is_finite(lang: Lang) -> Bool
      Whether the language has only finitely many words.

  fn count_words(lang: Lang, n: Int) -> Int
      How many words of length exactly `n` the language has.

  fn words_up_to(lang: Lang, n: Int) -> Set[Str]
      The words of the language of length at most `n`, as a set; there may be at
      most 100000 of them.

Call them as std.automata.<name>(...), or import them with `from std.automata
import <name>`.


File: kstar.info,  Node: std benchmark,  Next: std binary,  Prev: std automata,  Up: Standard library

std.benchmark
*************

How much work the algorithms do: counts that are the same on every computer, and
times in microseconds.

  fn equivalence_rounds(m: Lang) -> Int
      The rounds of refinement minimising `m`'s DFA takes: the smallest k for
      which k-equivalence equals (k+1)-equivalence.

  fn pda_configs(p: Pda, w: Str) -> Int
      How many configurations the search for an accepting run of `p` on `w`
      looks at.

  fn regex_size(m: Lang) -> Int
      The number of symbols and operators in the regex state elimination builds
      for `m`, as `to_regex` gives it.

  fn time_accepts_us(m: Acceptor, w: Str) -> Int
      Microseconds to decide whether `m` accepts `w`, the median of 5 runs; 0
      with a fixed clock.

  fn time_us(op: Str, m: Lang) -> Int
      Microseconds the operation `op` (`to_dfa`, `minimize`, `to_regex`,
      `remove_eps`, `complement` or `reverse`) takes on `m`, the median of 5
      runs; 0 with a fixed clock.

Call them as std.benchmark.<name>(...), or import them with `from std.benchmark
import <name>`.


File: kstar.info,  Node: std binary,  Next: std collections,  Prev: std benchmark,  Up: Standard library

std.binary
**********

Binary numbers and bit strings over {0, 1}: divisibility and remainders,
numerals without leading zeros, fixed widths, parity, runs of equal bits, and
converting between Ints and bit strings.

  fn divisible_by(n: Int) -> Dfa
      The bit strings whose value is a multiple of `n`, as the machine with one
      state per remainder.

  fn remainder(n: Int, r: Int) -> Dfa
      The bit strings whose value leaves remainder `r` when divided by `n`.

  fn no_leading_zeros() -> Dfa
      Numerals written without leading zeros: `0`, or a `1` followed by any
      bits.

  fn fixed_width(n: Int) -> Dfa
      The bit strings of exactly `n` bits.

  fn even_parity() -> Dfa
      The bit strings with an even number of 1s.

  fn odd_parity() -> Dfa
      The bit strings with an odd number of 1s.

  fn run_at_most(bit: Str, k: Int) -> Dfa
      The bit strings in which `bit` never appears more than `k` times in a row.

  fn to_binary(n: Int) -> Str
      `n` written in binary without leading zeros; `n` must not be negative, and
      0 is `"0"`.

  fn from_binary(bits: Str) -> Int
      The value of a bit string, most significant bit first; leading zeros do
      not count and `""` is 0.

Call them as std.binary.<name>(...), or import them with `from std.binary import
<name>`.


File: kstar.info,  Node: std collections,  Next: std grammar,  Prev: std binary,  Up: Standard library

std.collections
***************

Sets: membership, subsets, union, intersection, difference, size, smallest,
largest and sum, and every word of a given length over an alphabet.

  fn contains(s: Set[T], x: T) -> Bool
      Whether `x` is a member of the set `s`.

  fn difference(a: Set[T], b: Set[T]) -> Set[T]
      The members of the set `a` that are not in `b`.

  fn intersect(a: Set[T], b: Set[T]) -> Set[T]
      The members that the sets `a` and `b` have in common.

  fn is_subset(a: Set[T], b: Set[T]) -> Bool
      Whether every member of the set `a` is also in `b`; the empty set is a
      subset of every set.

  fn max(s: Set[Int]) -> Int
      The largest member of a set of Ints; the set must not be empty.

  fn min(s: Set[Int]) -> Int
      The smallest member of a set of Ints; the set must not be empty.

  fn size(s: Set[T]) -> Int
      The number of members of the set `s`; the same as `len(s)`.

  fn sum(s: Set[Int]) -> Int
      The members of a set of Ints added up; the sum of the empty set is 0.

  fn union(a: Set[T], b: Set[T]) -> Set[T]
      The members that are in the set `a`, in `b`, or in both.

  fn words(alphabet: Alphabet, n: Int) -> Set[Str]
      Every string of length `n` over `alphabet`, as a set; `n` must not be
      negative, and there may be at most 100000 of them.

  fn sigma_star(alphabet: Alphabet, n: Int) -> Set[Str]
      Σ* up to length `n`: every string over `alphabet` of length 0 to `n`, the
      empty string included.

  fn sigma_plus(alphabet: Alphabet, n: Int) -> Set[Str]
      Σ+ up to length `n`: every string over `alphabet` of length 1 to `n`; Σ*
      without the empty string.

  fn concat(a: Set[Str], b: Set[Str]) -> Set[Str]
      The concatenation of two sets of strings: every `xy` with `x` in `a` and
      `y` in `b`.

  fn power(a: Set[Str], n: Int) -> Set[Str]
      `a` to the power `n`: every string made of `n` strings of `a` one after
      another; the power 0 is the set holding the empty string.

  fn star_up_to(a: Set[Str], n: Int) -> Set[Str]
      The Kleene star of a set of strings, up to length `n`: every string of
      length at most `n` made of strings of `a` one after another.

  fn power_set(s: Set[T]) -> Set[Set[T]]
      Every subset of `s`, the empty set and `s` included: 2^n of them for `n`
      members, with `s` of at most 16 members.

Call them as std.collections.<name>(...), or import them with `from
std.collections import <name>`.


File: kstar.info,  Node: std grammar,  Next: std io,  Prev: std collections,  Up: Standard library

std.grammar
***********

Helpers for context-free grammars: the steps of the CNF construction one at a
time, FIRST and FOLLOW sets, LL(1) and CNF checks, listing short words, and
ready-made grammars.

  fn nullable(g: Grammar) -> Set[Str]
      The variables that can derive the empty word.

  fn generating(g: Grammar) -> Set[Str]
      The variables that can derive some word of terminals.

  fn reachable(g: Grammar) -> Set[Str]
      The variables that can appear in a derivation from the start variable.

  fn remove_useless(g: Grammar) -> Grammar
      The same grammar without the variables that generate nothing or cannot be
      reached.

  fn remove_eps(g: Grammar) -> Grammar
      An equivalent grammar whose only eps rule, if any, is for a start variable
      that is on no right-hand side.

  fn remove_units(g: Grammar) -> Grammar
      An equivalent grammar without unit rules such as `A -> B`.

  fn is_cnf(g: Grammar) -> Bool
      Whether the grammar is in Chomsky normal form, as `to_cnf` makes it.

  fn first(g: Grammar, variable: Str) -> Set[Str]
      The terminals that can begin a word `variable` derives, and `""` if it can
      derive the empty word.

  fn follow(g: Grammar, variable: Str) -> Set[Str]
      The terminals that can come right after `variable`, and `""` if it can
      come last.

  fn is_ll1(g: Grammar) -> Bool
      Whether a parser can always choose the rule to use by looking at the next
      symbol only.

  fn words_up_to(g: Grammar, n: Int) -> Set[Str]
      The words of the grammar's language of length at most `n`, as a set.

  fn parse_trees(g: Grammar, w: Str) -> Set[Str]
      The parse trees of `w`, each written with brackets as `S(a S(eps) b)`; at
      most 100, leaving out trees that go round a cycle of unit rules.

  fn is_ambiguous_on(g: Grammar, w: Str) -> Bool
      Whether `w` has two different parse trees (two different leftmost
      derivations) in the grammar.

  fn two_derivations(g: Grammar, w: Str) -> Str
      Two different leftmost derivations of `w`, showing that the grammar is
      ambiguous; `w` must have two parse trees.

  fn balanced(open: Str, close: Str) -> Grammar
      Balanced brackets: `S -> open S close S` or eps.

  fn anbn(a: Str, b: Str) -> Grammar
      `n` copies of `a` then `n` copies of `b`: `S -> a S b` or eps.

  fn palindromes(alphabet: Alphabet) -> Grammar
      The words over `alphabet` that read the same backwards.

  fn equal_count(a: Str, b: Str) -> Grammar
      The words over `a` and `b` with as many of one as of the other.

Call them as std.grammar.<name>(...), or import them with `from std.grammar
import <name>`.


File: kstar.info,  Node: std io,  Next: std math,  Prev: std grammar,  Up: Standard library

std.io
******

Reading and writing text files in the program's folder, such as word lists and
machines saved as JSON.

  fn append_text(path: Str, text: Str) -> Int
      Adds `text` to the end of the file at `path`, creating it if needed, and
      gives the number of bytes written.

  fn exists(path: Str) -> Bool
      Whether there is a file at `path`; a path outside the allowed folders is
      an error, not `false`.

  fn files(folder: Str) -> Set[Str]
      The paths of the files directly in `folder` (`"."` for the program's own),
      leaving out hidden ones.

  fn read_lines(path: Str) -> Set[Str]
      The lines of the file at `path` that are not empty, as a set.

  fn read_text(path: Str) -> Str
      The whole file at `path`, which must be UTF-8 text of at most 1 MiB; line
      ends become `\n`.

  fn read_words(path: Str) -> Set[Str]
      The words of the file at `path`, separated by spaces or new lines, as a
      set; a `#` starts a comment to the end of its line.

  fn write_lines(path: Str, lines: Set[Str]) -> Int
      Writes the members of `lines` to the file at `path`, one per line in the
      set's order, and gives the number of bytes written.

  fn write_text(path: Str, text: Str) -> Int
      Creates or replaces the file at `path` with `text`, and gives the number
      of bytes written.

Call them as std.io.<name>(...), or import them with `from std.io import
<name>`.


File: kstar.info,  Node: std math,  Next: std pda,  Prev: std io,  Up: Standard library

std.math
********

Whole-number arithmetic: division, powers, roots, divisors and primes.

  fn abs(n: Int) -> Int
      The size of `n`, without its sign.

  fn div(a: Int, b: Int) -> Int
      `a` divided by `b` as a whole number: the `q` with `a == b * q + a % b`.

  fn factorial(n: Int) -> Int
      `n! = 1 * 2 * ... * n`, for `n` from 0 to 20.

  fn gcd(a: Int, b: Int) -> Int
      The greatest common divisor of `a` and `b`; never negative, and `gcd(0,
      0)` is 0.

  fn is_prime(n: Int) -> Bool
      Whether `n` is a prime number: greater than 1, with no divisors but 1 and
      itself.

  fn lcm(a: Int, b: Int) -> Int
      The least common multiple of `a` and `b`; never negative, and 0 if either
      is 0.

  fn log2(n: Int) -> Int
      The base-2 logarithm of `n`, rounded down; `n` must be positive.

  fn max(a: Int, b: Int) -> Int
      The larger of `a` and `b`.

  fn min(a: Int, b: Int) -> Int
      The smaller of `a` and `b`.

  fn pow(base: Int, exp: Int) -> Int
      `base` to the power `exp`; `exp` must not be negative.

  fn sqrt(n: Int) -> Int
      The square root of `n`, rounded down; `n` must not be negative.

Call them as std.math.<name>(...), or import them with `from std.math import
<name>`.


File: kstar.info,  Node: std pda,  Next: std random,  Prev: std math,  Up: Standard library

std.pda
*******

Ready-made pushdown automata, the grammar of a PDA, whether a PDA is
deterministic, and membership and word lists that never give up.

  fn anbn(a: Str, b: Str) -> Pda
      A deterministic PDA for `n` copies of `a` then `n` copies of `b`.

  fn balanced(open: Str, close: Str) -> Pda
      A deterministic PDA for balanced brackets, with `open` and `close` as the
      brackets.

  fn palindromes(alphabet: Alphabet) -> Pda
      A PDA for the words over `alphabet` that read the same backwards; it
      guesses where the middle is.

  fn equal_count(a: Str, b: Str) -> Pda
      A PDA for the words over `a` and `b` with as many of one as of the other.

  fn to_grammar(p: Pda) -> Grammar
      A grammar with the same language as the PDA, by the triple construction.

  fn is_deterministic(p: Pda) -> Bool
      Whether at most one move can be taken in every configuration.

  fn stack_symbols(p: Pda) -> Set[Str]
      The symbols the PDA uses on its stack, the bottom symbol included.

  fn member(p: Pda, w: Str) -> Bool
      Whether the PDA accepts `w`, decided through its grammar, so it never
      gives up as `accepts` can.

  fn words_up_to(p: Pda, n: Int) -> Set[Str]
      The words the PDA accepts of length at most `n`, as a set.

  fn to_empty_stack(p: Pda) -> Pda
      A PDA that accepts by empty stack the words `p` accepts, by the usual
      construction; `p` itself if it already accepts by empty stack.

  fn to_final_state(p: Pda) -> Pda
      A PDA that accepts by final state the words `p` accepts, by the usual
      construction; `p` itself if it already accepts by final state.

  fn ids(p: Pda, w: Str) -> Str
      The run of `p` on `w` as instantaneous descriptions, `(q, w, stack) ⊢ (q',
      w', stack')`, one per line, then the result.

Call them as std.pda.<name>(...), or import them with `from std.pda import
<name>`.


File: kstar.info,  Node: std random,  Next: std regex,  Prev: std pda,  Up: Standard library

std.random
**********

Random Ints, words, DFAs, NFAs and grammars from a seed: the same seed always
gives the same result.

  fn int(seed: Int, low: Int, high: Int) -> Int
      An Int from `low` to `high`, both included, each as likely.

  fn pick(seed: Int, s: Set[T]) -> T
      A member of the set `s`, each as likely; the set must not be empty.

  fn word(seed: Int, alphabet: Alphabet, n: Int) -> Str
      A word of length `n` over `alphabet`, each symbol drawn on its own.

  fn words(seed: Int, alphabet: Alphabet, count: Int, max: Int) -> Set[Str]
      `count` random words of length 0 to `max`, as a set, so fewer if some are
      drawn twice.

  fn word_in(seed: Int, lang: Lang, n: Int) -> Str
      A word of length `n` in the language, each such word as likely; there must
      be one.

  fn random_dfa(seed: Int, alphabet: Alphabet, states: Int) -> Dfa
      A random DFA with `states` states named `q0`, `q1`, ...

  fn random_nfa(seed: Int, alphabet: Alphabet, states: Int) -> Nfa
      A random NFA with `states` states named `q0`, `q1`, ..., which may have
      eps moves.

  fn random_grammar(seed: Int, alphabet: Alphabet, variables: Int) -> Grammar
      A random grammar with `variables` variables, from 1 to 26, named `S`, `A`,
      `B`, ...

Call them as std.random.<name>(...), or import them with `from std.random import
<name>`.


File: kstar.info,  Node: std regex,  Next: std steps,  Prev: std random,  Up: Standard library

std.regex
*********

Regular expressions built at run time and put together from pieces: from text,
from a word or an alphabet, by choice, sequence and repetition, and the common
prefix, suffix and substring forms.

  fn parse(text: Str) -> Regex
      The regex written in `text`, in the same syntax as between slashes; text
      that is not a regex is an error.

  fn literal(word: Str) -> Regex
      The regex that matches exactly `word` and nothing else.

  fn any_of(alphabet: Alphabet) -> Regex
      The regex for any one symbol of `alphabet`, written out as a choice
      between them.

  fn alt(a: Regex, b: Regex) -> Regex
      The choice of `a` or `b`: the words of either one.

  fn seq(a: Regex, b: Regex) -> Regex
      `ab`: a word of `a` followed by a word of `b`.

  fn zero_or_more(r: Regex) -> Regex
      `r*`: any number of words of `r` one after another, including none.

  fn one_or_more(r: Regex) -> Regex
      `r+`: one or more words of `r` one after another.

  fn optional(r: Regex) -> Regex
      `r?`: a word of `r`, or the empty string.

  fn power(r: Regex, n: Int) -> Regex
      Exactly `n` words of `r` one after another; `power(r, 0)` matches only the
      empty string.

  fn repeat_between(r: Regex, min: Int, max: Int) -> Regex
      From `min` to `max` words of `r` one after another, both included.

  fn at_least(r: Regex, n: Int) -> Regex
      `n` or more words of `r` one after another.

  fn starts_with(alphabet: Alphabet, prefix: Str) -> Regex
      The regex for the strings over `alphabet` that begin with `prefix`.

  fn ends_with(alphabet: Alphabet, suffix: Str) -> Regex
      The regex for the strings over `alphabet` that end with `suffix`.

  fn contains(alphabet: Alphabet, part: Str) -> Regex
      The regex for the strings over `alphabet` that contain `part` somewhere.

  fn size(r: Regex) -> Int
      The number of parts of the regex: its symbols, operators and joins.

  fn text(r: Regex) -> Str
      The regex written out as a string, without the slashes.

  fn by_arden(m: Lang) -> Regex
      A regex for the language of `m` found by Arden's theorem, as
      `std.steps.arden` shows; the same language as `to_regex(m)`, often written
      differently.

Call them as std.regex.<name>(...), or import them with `from std.regex import
<name>`.


File: kstar.info,  Node: std steps,  Next: std string,  Prev: std regex,  Up: Standard library

std.steps
*********

The working of the conversions, step by step: eps-closures, the subset
construction table, the k-equivalence partitions, state elimination and Arden's
theorem.

  fn arden(m: Lang) -> Str
      From a finite automaton to a regex by Arden's theorem: an equation for
      each state, then each solved with R = Q + RP giving R = QP* and put into
      the others, then the regex.

  fn elimination(m: Lang) -> Str
      State elimination, as `to_regex` does it: the new start and final states,
      then each state removed and the edges that replace the paths through it,
      then the regex.

  fn eps_closures(m: Lang) -> Str
      A table of each state of `m`'s NFA and its eps-closure, the states it
      reaches by eps moves alone.

  fn partitions(m: Lang) -> Str
      Minimisation of `m`'s DFA by k-equivalence: the partitions P0, P1, ...
      until one is the same as the one before.

  fn subset_table(m: Lang) -> Str
      The subset construction for `m`'s NFA as a table: each set of states
      found, in order, and where each symbol leads, with the empty set written
      Ø.

Call them as std.steps.<name>(...), or import them with `from std.steps import
<name>`.


File: kstar.info,  Node: std string,  Next: std testing,  Prev: std steps,  Up: Standard library

std.string
**********

Words as data: searching, slicing, repeating, and the prefixes, suffixes and
substrings of a word.

  fn char_at(s: Str, i: Int) -> Str
      The character of `s` at index `i`, counting from 0, as a string of length
      1.

  fn contains(s: Str, part: Str) -> Bool
      Whether `part` occurs anywhere inside `s`; the empty string occurs in
      every string.

  fn count(s: Str, part: Str) -> Int
      How many times `part` occurs in `s`, not overlapping: `count("aaaa",
      "aa")` is 2; `part` must not be empty.

  fn ends_with(s: Str, suffix: Str) -> Bool
      Whether `s` ends with `suffix`.

  fn from_int(n: Int) -> Str
      The digits of `n` as a string, with a leading `-` if it is negative.

  fn index_of(s: Str, part: Str) -> Int
      The index of the first occurrence of `part` in `s`, or -1 if there is
      none.

  fn is_palindrome(s: Str) -> Bool
      Whether `s` reads the same backwards; the empty string does.

  fn length(s: Str) -> Int
      The number of characters in `s`; the same as `len(s)`.

  fn prefixes(s: Str) -> Set[Str]
      Every prefix of `s`, including the empty string and `s` itself.

  fn repeat(s: Str, n: Int) -> Str
      `s` written `n` times in a row; `n` must not be negative, and the result
      at most 1000000 characters.

  fn reverse(s: Str) -> Str
      The characters of `s` in the opposite order.

  fn starts_with(s: Str, prefix: Str) -> Bool
      Whether `s` starts with `prefix`.

  fn substring(s: Str, start: Int, end: Int) -> Str
      The characters of `s` from index `start` up to, but not including, index
      `end`; needs `0 <= start <= end <= length(s)`.

  fn substrings(s: Str) -> Set[Str]
      Every substring of `s` (a run of neighbouring characters), including the
      empty string and `s` itself.

  fn suffixes(s: Str) -> Set[Str]
      Every suffix of `s`, including the empty string and `s` itself.

  fn symbols(s: Str) -> Set[Str]
      The distinct characters of `s`, each as a string of length 1; the empty
      string is not one of them.

  fn to_int(s: Str) -> Int
      The number written in `s`: digits with an optional leading `-` or `+`, and
      nothing else.

Call them as std.string.<name>(...), or import them with `from std.string import
<name>`.


