std.testing
***********

Checks for `test` statements that explain a failure: equality with the
difference shown, sets of words a machine must accept or reject, equal languages
with a word that tells them apart, and two acceptors that must agree on sample
words.

  fn equal(expected: T, actual: T) -> Bool
      Whether `actual` is `expected`; a failing test shows both, with the first
      difference of two strings marked.

  fn equal_sets(expected: Set[T], actual: Set[T]) -> Bool
      Whether two sets are equal; a failing test lists the members that are
      missing and those that should not be there.

  fn accepts_all(machine: Acceptor, words: Set[Str]) -> Bool
      Whether `machine` accepts every word of `words`; a failing test lists the
      words it rejects.

  fn rejects_all(machine: Acceptor, words: Set[Str]) -> Bool
      Whether `machine` rejects every word of `words`; a failing test lists the
      words it accepts.

  fn same_language(expected: Lang, actual: Lang) -> Bool
      Whether two regular languages are `equivalent`; a failing test shows a
      shortest word that is in one but not the other.

  fn agree_on(a: Acceptor, b: Acceptor, words: Set[Str]) -> Bool
      Whether `a` and `b` accept the same words of `words`; a failing test lists
      the words only one of them accepts.

  fn agree_up_to(a: Acceptor, b: Acceptor, n: Int) -> Bool
      Whether `a` and `b` accept the same words of length up to `n` over their
      symbols, at most 10000 words; a failing test lists the words only one
      accepts.

Call them as std.testing.<name>(...), or import them with `from std.testing
import <name>`.


File: kstar.info,  Node: std tm,  Next: std visual,  Prev: std testing,  Up: Standard library

std.tm
******

Ready-made Turing machines (binary increment, unary addition and copying,
deciders for palindromes and a^n b^n c^n), and the steps, space and final state
of a run.

  fn increment() -> Tm
      A machine that adds one to a binary number, leaving the sum on the tape.

  fn unary_add() -> Tm
      A machine that turns `m` ones, a 0 and `n` ones into `m + n` ones.

  fn unary_copy() -> Tm
      A machine that turns `n` ones into `n` ones, a 0 and `n` ones again.

  fn palindromes(alphabet: Alphabet) -> Tm
      A machine that decides the palindromes over `alphabet`, halting in `yes`
      or `no`.

  fn anbncn(a: Str, b: Str, c: Str) -> Tm
      A machine that decides `n` copies of `a`, then of `b`, then of `c`, which
      no PDA can.

  fn steps(m: Tm, w: Str) -> Int
      How many steps the machine takes on `w` before it halts.

  fn space(m: Tm, w: Str) -> Int
      How many tape cells the head visits on `w` before the machine halts.

  fn final_state(m: Tm, w: Str) -> Str
      The state the machine is in when it halts on `w`.

  fn halts_within(m: Tm, w: Str, n: Int) -> Bool
      Whether the machine halts on `w` within `n` steps, at most 100000.

  fn tape_symbols(m: Tm) -> Set[Str]
      The symbols that can be on the tape: the alphabet, the blank `_`, and
      every symbol the machine writes.

  fn ids(m: Tm, w: Str) -> Str
      The run of `m` on `w` as instantaneous descriptions `u q v` (the head on
      the first symbol of `v`), one per line after `⊢`, then how it halted; at
      most 100 steps.

Call them as std.tm.<name>(...), or import them with `from std.tm import
<name>`.


File: kstar.info,  Node: std visual,  Prev: std tm,  Up: Standard library

std.visual
**********

Drawings and traces as strings: DOT diagrams, a run drawn on its diagram, the
trace table, and the page that plays a run.

  fn dot(m: Acceptor) -> Str
      The Graphviz DOT text that `draw m` writes; a regex or set is drawn as its
      NFA.

  fn dot_run(m: Acceptor, w: Str) -> Str
      The DOT text of machine `m` with its run on `w` drawn on it: the states
      and moves used in bold, the states it ends in filled.

  fn player(m: Acceptor, w: Str) -> Str
      The self-contained HTML page that `draw m on w` writes, which plays the
      run step by step.

  fn trace_text(m: Acceptor, w: Str) -> Str
      The table `trace m on w` prints, as a string, with `-` in the empty cells.

Call them as std.visual.<name>(...), or import them with `from std.visual import
<name>`.


